This paper tackles the challenge of estimating the continuous-time state of power networks, which are modeled by complex, nonlinear equations. These networks rely on intricate power balance laws to define their dynamics, with measurements of phasors and voltage magnitudes, along with their angles, being the only inputs available for estimation. Initially, it's demonstrated that these networks can be simplified into a specific type of mathematical model, known as a differential algebraic equation. This simplified model is then analyzed using a specific algorithm, which is an extension of a well-known method for estimating states in systems. The algorithm, which is an extension of a well-known method, is shown to be effective in accurately estimating the states of these complex networks. Furthermore, it's found that this algorithm can also be applied to estimate the states of these networks from noisy data. Additionally, it's shown that this algorithm can handle a wide range of uncertainties in the measurements, which are an essential requirement for practical applications. Finally, it's demonstrated that this algorithm can effectively estimate the states of these networks in real-time, with results from simulations on a specific power grid model supporting these findings. The corresponding code for this research is available online for reference. 

